<</Ff 8392704/BS <</S/S/W 1>>/T(Text Field50)/Rect[66.812 52.175 566.598 565.894]/V(Environmental Monitoring \(EM\) Recoveries Review:\r \rISO 5 BSC and Personnel Evaluation:\rUpon review of the environmental monitoring data, absence of microbial recovery \(No Growth\) was demonstrated across all critical ISO 5 zones and personnel monitoring on the date of testing. Daily personnel monitoring for processing analyst Ellen Bennett and changeover analyst Karla Silva yielded no microbial growth on left and right fingertip touch plates. Daily surface sampling \(4 locations\) and settling plates within processing BSC E001938 and changeover BSC E001937 yielded no microbial growth.\r \rCleanroom EM Evaluation:\rWeekly surface monitoring of Cleanroom L-Suite conducted on 01Oct26 demonstrated no microbial recovery across all sampled cleanroom surfaces, confirming effective sanitization and surface control.\r \rWeekly active air monitoring of Cleanroom L-Suite conducted on 01Oct26 demonstrated no microbial recovery in ISO 7 cleanroom 145 and ISO 8 anteroom 143; however, 1 CFU under test sample ETX-261012-0430 was recovered from ISO 7 cleanroom 144 Section II \(identified as Staphylococcus epidermidis\), and 5 CFUs under test sample ETX-261012-0433 were recovered from outermost ISO 8 room 142 \(identified as Staphylococcus epidermidis, Pseudomonas parafulva, Bacillus safensis, Corynebacterium xerosis, and Corynebacterium ureicelerivorans\). The recovered organisms are common environmental isolates with morphology distinctly different from the Cocci shaped morphology observed in the failing test sample, and no microbial match could be established.\r \rIt is important to emphasize that all sample processing activities were performed strictly within the validated ISO 5 BSC E001938 and changeover activities within ISO 5 BSC E001937, both located in the ISO 7 cleanroom \(144\). There is minimal exposure of the test sample to ambient ISO 8 air, as samples and supplies are transferred in disinfected, closed containers on carts through the layered cleanroom suites. Furthermore, the recoveries occurred in the general cleanroom and lower-classified ISO 8 outer room environments, which are physically segregated from the ISO 5 processing zone by closed doors and an outward-cascading positive air pressure gradient. In conjunction with the complete absence of microbial growth on all analyst glove touch plates, settling plates, and ISO 5 BSC work surfaces throughout testing, there is no plausible contamination transfer pathway from the ambient environment to the test sample.\r \rMonthly cleaning and disinfection of the cleanrooms \(ISO 7\) and their containing biosafety cabinets \(ISO 5\) were performed on 27Sep26 for L-Suite, as per MICRO-SOP-9, Cleaning and Disinfecting Procedure for Microbiology. It was documented that all chemical indicators passed, verifying that environmental sanitization remained validated and in control prior to testing.\r \rAnalyzing a 6-month sample history for Link Pharmacy \(E19112\), this specific analyte "Tirzepatide/Levocarnitine 17mg/100mg/mL \(1ml\)" has had no prior Out-of-Specification \(OOS\) occurrences under ScanRDI sterility testing.\r \rAll samples processed on the same day were also evaluated for evidence of sample-to-sample cross-contamination. The test sample ETX-260929-0931 was the 39th sample processed by Ellen Bennett during the 2nd Shift on 01Oct26 within BSC E001938 \(Cleanroom 144, L-Suite\). All other samples processed by Ellen Bennett during this shift yielded negative results. Review of laboratory records identified one other sample that yielded a positive result on 01Oct26, documented under OOS-262285 for CasaPharma Rx \(E72585\), sample ETX-260930-0635 \(Tirzepatide/B12 15mg/1mg/0.5mL - 2mL\). However, that sample was processed by America Alanis \(11th sample processed\) during the 1st Shift. The two testing events were segregated by shift, processing analyst and primary containment equipment. Furthermore, analyst Ellen Bennett verified that gloves were disinfected between samples and standard aseptic techniques were strictly maintained. Consequently, there is no evidence of sample-to-sample cross-contamination or a shared laboratory vector.\r \rBased on the laboratory investigation, no assignable laboratory cause was identified involving the analyst, instrument, reagents, supplies, or monitored laboratory environment. Therefore, the likelihood of laboratory error contributing to this failure is considered minimal, and the original result is deemed valid.)/F 4/Subtype/Widget/Type/Annot/M(D:20250930131316-06'00')/DA(0 0 0 rg /Helv 7.3 Tf)/MK <<>>/FT/Tx/NM(4aa3f251-1e4a-4411-83ff-19fe795047ec)/AP <</N 603 0 R>>>>
endobj
